# Local-only test harness (not part of the release)
tests/

# Local-only assurance-ladder infrastructure. The public surface is
# `giz --self-check` (and the runtime guards it exercises); the
# internal toolchain that produces those guarantees is intentionally
# kept private:
#   * scripts/redteam/   isolated + live attacker scenarios
#   * .github/           CI workflows that run the full ladder
#   * Makefile           developer convenience targets
#   * THREAT_MODEL.md    cross-references the local test files
#   * dev-requirements.txt  pytest / hypothesis / ruff / bandit / pyright
#
# This is a deliberate decision: the public repo ships the runtime
# code and a runnable self-check; the maintainer's lab keeps the
# adversarial toolchain off the index page.
scripts/
.github/
Makefile
THREAT_MODEL.md
dev-requirements.txt
pytest.ini
ruff.toml

# Scanner raw outputs stay local. Only the human-readable SUMMARY.md
# is checked into the public tree. Three reasons this is a per-pattern
# allowlist rather than a single "security-scans/**" block:
#   1. Several tools (Safety, Grype, Syft, Trivy in some modes) embed
#      absolute filesystem paths from the maintainer's machine into
#      their output. Those paths are personal information leakage
#      and were the bug that triggered this rule in v0.2.6.
#   2. SBOM .json / .spdx files are large, opaque, and noisy in
#      git diffs; reviewers cannot eyeball them anyway.
#   3. The SUMMARY.md is the only artifact that's actually useful to
#      a casual auditor; it points at the exact tool versions and
#      flags so anyone can reproduce.
security-scans/**/*.json
security-scans/**/*.sarif
security-scans/**/*.spdx
security-scans/**/*.cyclonedx
security-scans/**/*.txt
security-scans/**/codeql.sarif
security-scans/**/trivy.json
security-scans/**/checkov.json
security-scans/**/pyright.json
security-scans/**/yara.txt
security-scans/**/graudit.txt
pyrightconfig.json
